{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '300,365p;1320,1435p'; rg -n -C 4 'make-woken-run-queue-entries|terminal-resume-outcome|:dao.stream/refused|:status :refused' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   300\t(defn attach-resource\n   301\t  \"Install the host stream `handle` a composition hands a task under a\n   302\t   fresh resource id, and return `[stream-ref state]` with the reference\n   303\t   sealed under the task's secret. This is how a stream reaches a program:\n   304\t   never through the store.\"\n   305\t  [state handle]\n   306\t  (let [[id state] (gensym state \"stream\")\n   307\t        ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)]\n   308\t    [ref (assoc-in state [:resources id] handle)]))\n   309\t\n   310\t\n   311\t(defn make-woken-run-queue-entries\n   312\t  \"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\n   313\t   Readers (with :cursor-ref) record the successor cursor the transport\n   314\t   returned as a resource update. Writers (no :cursor-ref) just stamp\n   315\t   :value. Each woken result's :status rides onto the ready entry, where\n   316\t   terminal-resume-outcome reads it.\n   317\t\n   318\t   A woken refused status is shaped into the same refusal value the\n   319\t   immediate `handle-put`/`handle-next` return, so whether the first\n   320\t   attempt blocked does not change what the program sees.\n   321\t\n   322\t   The ready entry stays pure data: any `:stream` handle a poll resolved\n   323\t   is dropped -- the resource updates carry the successor cursor, and\n   324\t   handles are re-resolved from ids when needed again -- so an entry\n   325\t   neither waits nor runs holding a host object.\"\n   326\t  [state woken]\n   327\t  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor status], :as woken-entry}]\n   328\t          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\n   329\t                value (if (= :dao.stream/refused status)\n   330\t                        (cond-> {:status :refused\n   331\t                                 :outcome status\n   332\t                                 :stream-id (:stream-id entry)}\n   333\t                          cursor-ref (assoc :cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)))\n   334\t                        value)\n   335\t                updates\n   336\t                (or (:resource-updates woken-entry)\n   337\t                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\n   338\t                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   339\t                            cursor-data (get (:resources state) cursor-id)]\n   340\t                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\n   341\t            (dissoc (assoc entry\n   342\t                           :value value\n   343\t                           :resource-updates updates\n   344\t                           :status status\n   345\t                           :cursor cursor)\n   346\t                    :stream)))\n   347\t        woken))\n   348\t\n   349\t\n   350\t(defn handle-make\n   351\t  \"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\n   352\t   `:make-stream`, holds the handle in the private `:resources` table, and\n   353\t   answers a sealed reference; the reference is issued first, so a task\n   354\t   with no secret creates no stream.\n   355\t\n   356\t   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\n   357\t   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\n   358\t   silently falling back to a private transport is the failure mode the rule\n   359\t   exists to prevent.\n   360\t   Returns [stream-ref updated-state].\"\n   361\t  [state effect id]\n   362\t  (let [capacity (or (:capacity effect) vm/default-stream-capacity)\n   363\t        stream-ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)\n   364\t        handle (vm/create-stream! (:make-stream state) capacity :stream/make)]\n   365\t    [stream-