m-id id}` (the effect's value, mirroring `poll-link-response`), while `closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` keep their documented v1 throws via an explicit branch. `:448-458` — `handle-next` same shape plus `:cursor-id`. `:329-334` — `make-woken-run-queue-entries` shapes a woken refused status into the identical refusal value; `:1356-1372` — `terminal-resume-outcome` admits `:dao.stream/refused`, keeping the \"whether the first attempt blocked must not change that\" law true; both docstrings updated.\n- `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:709-716` — the `:refused` clause mirroring `:defect` (`:signal :dao.stream/refused`, `:member i`, `:result read`, cursor unchanged, `:next` moves past); docstrings at 636 (\"eight outcomes\") and 648-649 (refused in the k enumeration) only.\n\n### Test changes\n\n- Pinned tables widened with actual semantics: `test/dao/stream/observe_test.cljc` (read table gains `:refused`, effect table `:failed`), `test/dao/stream/waitset_test.cljc:190,203` (both plans: refused wakes terminal under its own keyword, advance 0), `test/dao/jing_test.cljc:808` (pool-signals refused row).\n- Conformance manifests gain refused as an exclusion with a reason (the in-memory backends compose no policy, so they cannot produce it): `test/dao/stream/ringbuffer_test.cljc:99-113`, `test/dao/stream/memory_log_test.cljc:189-217`, and both stream_test fixtures (`conformance-manifest-validation-test`, `conformance-harness-full-run-test`).\n- Exact-set assertions updated in `test/dao/stream_test.cljc:244-264`.\n- New tests: `refused-outcome-is-declared-for-cursor-next-append-test` (stream_test.cljc:279), `a-refused-read-is-its-own-refused-status` (observe_test.cljc:76), `refused-append-answers-are-shaped-refusals-test`, `refused-read-answers-are-shaped-refusals-test`, `a-woken-refused-entry-resumes-as-data-test` (engine_test.cljc:158, 225, 468) — pinning the shaped refusal (immediate and woken paths), unrecognized-as-refused, cursor-cell immobility, and that the declared defects still throw.\n\n### Verification (sequential, solo, under mise)\n\n- cljstyle check: exit 0 on all 11 files. clj-kondo: 0 errors; 3 warnings, verified byte-identical on HEAD (pre-existing).\n- JVM: `clojure -M:test` — Ran 2,214 tests containing 182,863 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- Node: `bb test:cljs` (peer built first) — Ran 2,126 tests containing 49,532 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- Dart: `bb test:cljd` — 2,088 tests, All tests passed!\n\nNote on counts: the measured JVM total is 2,214/182,863 against the brief's quoted baseline of 2,057/180,912; the totals were identical across two full runs of this tree, so the quoted baseline is stale for d81e50ad — the deltas are not from this slice (it adds 5 test vars).\n\nStatus: COMPLETE","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc | sed -n '1,165p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '675,730p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '105,128p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.observe\n     2\t  \"One observation step over a DaoStream reader: read a value, act on it, and\n     3\t   advance the cursor only if the act succeeded.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `step` owns no scheduler, loop, state, callback or policy.  It classifies\n     6\t   every outcome the contract declares as data and hands the caller what it\n     7\t   needs to decide: the successor cursor on progress, the recovery cursor on a\n     8\t   gap, the raw answer on a defect.  What to do about a gap, whether a defect\n     9\t   is fatal, how many values to take per turn, and what state to carry between\n    10\t   turns all belong to the caller.\n    11\t\n    12\t   The cursor advances in exactly one place -- after the effect has answered\n    13\t   `ok` -- so effect-before-commit is a property of this seam rather than a\n    14\t   rule each caller keeps.  An effect that throws propagates before any\n    15\t   successor exists, which gives a throwing effect the same guarantee for\n    16\t   free.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Callers are `dao.stream.forward` (effect: append to another stream),\n    19\t   `dao.stream.observer` (effect: load a program into a VM) and\n    20\t   `dao.jing` (effect: materialize content).  Each adds its own loop and its\n    21\t   own policy above this step.\n    22\t\n    23\t   The law every interpreter here obeys is that an element's cursor advances\n    24\t   exactly when its disposition has been durably recorded.  This step serves\n    25\t   the interpreters whose disposition is an external, refusable effect -- an\n    26\t   append, a load, a materialization -- where that means advancing only after\n    27\t   the effect answered ok.  Interpreters whose disposition is a local, total\n    28\t   state transition keep their own read loops: `dao.stream.rpc/poll!`,\n    29\t   `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` and `yin.vm.engine`'s wait set thread\n    30\t   whole caller state under a budget with terminal short-circuits, and for\n    31\t   them advancing first and advancing after the commit are the same fact,\n    32\t   because no window exists in which one happened and the other did not.\n    33\t   `serve-once!` is the proof that this is one law and not two families: it\n    34\t   advances after delivery for a correlatable request, on the diagnostic for\n    35\t   an uncorrelatable one, and on terminal loss for an undeliverable response.\n    36\t\n    37\t   There is no ordering parameter here and none is needed.  A consumer that\n    38\t   wants an element consumed even when its interpretation is defective writes\n    39\t   a *total* effect -- one that classifies every element as data and so always\n    40\t   answers ok -- and gets consume-exactly-once through this same step.\"\n    41\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn- valid-or-transport-error\n    45\t  \"Fold a defective host answer into the contract's outcome algebra.\n    46\t\n    47\t   A conforming operation already returns one of the contract outcomes; this\n    48\t   keeps the step's branch total when a composition supplies a bad handle or\n    49\t   an effect answers with something that is not an outcome map.\"\n    50\t  [operation result]\n    51\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? operation result)\n    52\t    result\n    53\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    54\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result\n    55\t     :dao.stream/answer result}))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn step\n    59\t  \"Read one value from `source` at `cursor` and run `effect` on it.\n    60\t\n    61\t   `effect` is `(fn [value] -> outcome-map)` answering with the writer outcome\n    62\t   set: `ok`, `full` (\\\"not yet\\\"), `invalid-value`, `closed`, `refused`, or\n    63\t   `transport-error`.  Any other answer is classified as `transport-error`\n    64\t   with the raw answer retained.  An effect that throws propagates.\n    65\t\n    66\t   Returns a map carrying `:cursor` and `:read` (the raw read answer) in every\n    67\t   case:\n    68\t\n    69\t   | status     | when                                    | cursor    | also         |\n    70\t   | ---------- | --------------------------------------- | --------- | ------------ |\n    71\t   | `:advance` | read ok, effect ok                      | successor | `:effect`    |\n    72\t   | `:retry`   | read blocked, or effect full            | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    73\t   | `:ended`   | read end                                | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    74\t   | `:gap`     | read gap                                | unchanged | `:recovery`  |\n    75\t   | `:refused` | read refused, nothing observed\n    76\t   |            | unchanged | `:outcome`   |\n    77\t   | `:defect`  | read cursor-mismatch, invalid-cursor,\n    78\t                  transport-error, or malformed | unchanged | `:outcome` |\n    79\t   | `:failed`  | effect invalid-value, closed, refused,\n    80\t                  transport-error, or malformed | unchanged | `:outcome`,\n    81\t                  `:effect` |\n    82\t\n    83\t   A `:retry` from the effect also carries `:effect`.  The cursor is the\n    84\t   successor on `:advance` and unchanged on every other status.  `refused`\n    85\t   is a policy answer, not a defect: nothing was observed and nothing was\n    86\t   recorded, and the cursor stays where it was.\"\n    87\t  [source cursor effect]\n    88\t  (let [read-result (valid-or-transport-error :next (stream/next source cursor))\n    89\t        read-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n    90\t    (case read-outcome\n    91\t      :dao.stream/ok\n    92\t      (let [effect-result (valid-or-transport-error\n    93\t                            :append!\n    94\t                            (effect (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n    95\t            effect-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome effect-result)]\n    96\t        (case effect-outcome\n    97\t          :dao.stream/ok {:status :advance\n    98\t                          :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n    99\t                          :read read-result\n   100\t                          :effect effect-result}\n   101\t          :dao.stream/full {:status :retry\n   102\t                            :cursor cursor\n   103\t                            :outcome effect-outcome\n   104\t                            :read read-result\n   105\t                            :effect effect-result}\n   106\t          {:status :failed\n   107\t           :cursor cursor\n   108\t           :outcome effect-outcome\n   109\t           :read read-result\n   110\t           :effect effect-result}))\n   111\t\n   112\t      :dao.stream/blocked {:status :retry\n   113\t                           :cursor cursor\n   114\t                           :outcome read-outcome\n   115\t                           :read read-result}\n   116\t\n   117\t      :dao.stream/end {:status :ended\n   118\t                       :cursor cursor\n   119\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   120\t                       :read read-result}\n   121\t\n   122\t      :dao.stream/gap {:status :gap\n   123\t                       :cursor cursor\n   124\t                       :outcome read-outcome\n   125\t                       :recovery (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n   126\t                       :read read-result}\n   127\t\n   128\t      :dao.stream/refused {:status :refused\n   129\t                           :cursor cursor\n   130\t                           :outcome read-outcome\n   131\t                           :read read-result}\n   132\t\n   133\t      (:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n   134\t  