5\t                            (assoc :next (mod (inc i) n)))\n   706\t                 :signal :dao.stream/gap\n   707\t                 :member i\n   708\t                 :cursor (:recovery r)}\n   709\t                :refused\n   710\t                {:state (-> state'\n   711\t                            (assoc-in [:members i :status] (:outcome r))\n   712\t                            (assoc :next (mod (inc i) n)))\n   713\t                 :signal (:outcome r)\n   714\t                 :member i\n   715\t                 :result (:read r)}\n   716\t                :defect\n   717\t                {:state (-> state'\n   718\t                            (assoc-in [:members i :status] (:outcome r))\n   719\t                            (assoc :next (mod (inc i) n)))\n   720\t                 :signal (:outcome r)\n   721\t                 :member i\n   722\t                 :result (:read r)}\n   723\t                :failed\n   724\t                (throw (ex-info\n   725\t                         \"unreachable: the DaoJing effect answers ok or throws\"\n   726\t                         {:result r, :member i}))))))))))\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n   114\t  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   115\t  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n   116\t  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n   117\t  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n   118\t  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n   119\t  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n   120\t  say that asking again is the right response.\n   121\t- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n   122\t  produce: a policy composed on this handle declined the operation; nothing\n   123\t  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n   124\t  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\n   125\t  it: a name is not gated and a lifecycle transition is not declined. What\n   126\t  the policy is, and how it is composed, is outside this contract.\n   127\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   128\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
